## After the MVP (do not jump ahead)

Finish Days 1–7 first so you have a demable repo (skill curve, `forecast.json`, click→live impacts map, README). Only then stack these upgrades **in this order**:

### Stretch 1. Live cloud inference (AWS)

Export the CNN to ONNX. Put model, latest input, `forecast.json`, and `oni_monthly.csv` in S3. Serve forecast ONI and/or click impacts from Lambda behind API Gateway over HTTPS. Wire the map fully to cloud (no local uvicorn).

**Done when:** public HTTPS endpoints power the ONI readout and map clicks.

This is the production story. Prefer this over CMIP if you only have time for one stretch.

### Stretch 2. CMIP pretrain → fine-tune (Nature-paper path)

Pretrain the CNN on climate-model ENSO simulations (CMIP-scale synthetic history), then fine-tune on real ERSST + ONI. Re-run the skill curve honestly. Re-export ONNX and refresh the Lambda artifact.

**Done when:** test skill clearly improves vs the observations-only model at one or more leads, and the writeup names the transfer-learning setup and its limits.

Do Stretch 2 last: highest ML upside, also the heaviest data/compute lift. Do not block the MVP or Stretch 1 on it.

---

## The resume lines you are working toward

- Built ENSOcast, a convolutional neural network that forecasts the El Niño index from tropical Pacific ocean temperature maps, benchmarked against persistence across forecast lead times.
- Deployed the model as a serverless inference endpoint on AWS Lambda behind API Gateway, with artifacts in S3.
- Built an interactive map translating the forecast into plain language regional winter impacts with a per region confidence measure.

Optional later lines (after stretches):

- Added transfer learning from CMIP simulations to observational SST before fine-tuning the ONI forecaster.
- Served click-anywhere winter impacts live via API (Open-Meteo + ONI regression) behind API Gateway.

## If the week gets tight

Cut in this order, last thing first: Stretch 2 (CMIP), then Lambda (demo with local FastAPI), then precip/snow in the live impacts payload, then map polish, then extra forecast leads. Never cut the skill curve or the honest evaluation. Those are what make it a real project rather than a demo.
